[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dswa2, dswb2
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the DHCP relay function (isc-dhcp-relay/dhcrelay) on the distribution switches. Mechanism: With no relay running on the active gateway for a VLAN, client DHCPDISCOVER broadcasts are never forwarded to the DHCP server, so new clients time out. Existing clients continue working because they already have leases and default gateways.

Localization: On the A-side, dswa2 is VRRP master for user VLANs 20 and 40 (VIPs present as 10.2.0.1/32 on br0.20 and 10.6.0.1/32 on br0.40), but no dhcrelay process is running there. On the B-side, dswb2 is VRRP master for VLANs 20 and 30 (VIPs 10.4.0.1/32 on br0.20 and 10.5.0.1/32 on br0.30), and likewise no dhcrelay is running. On both switches, the isc-dhcp-relay service is inactive and /etc/default/isc-dhcp-relay is unconfigured (SERVERS="" and INTERFACES=""). In contrast, their peers (dswa1 and dswb1) do run dhcrelay pointing at 10.0.0.76 and list the client VLAN interfaces, and both can reach 10.0.0.76, confirming the server path is good.

Root cause: isc-dhcp-relay is not configured or running on dswa2 and dswb2, which became VRRP masters for several client VLANs after the distribution-switch change.

Corrective fix: Configure and enable the relay on both DSW peers. Set SERVERS="10.0.0.76" and INTERFACES="br0.10 br0.20 br0.30 br0.40 br0.99 eth5 eth6" (per site design), enable and start isc-dhcp-relay, or manage dhcrelay via keepalived notify scripts so the active VRRP node always runs the relay. This restores DHCP forwarding for new clients on all VLANs.
=== END ===